WEBVTT

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.167
[theme music playing]

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:19.753
[engine revving]

00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:30.100
[Jerry] <i>Really, what is the point </i>
<i>of a car like this?</i>

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:32.467
<i>It's small. It's impractical.</i>

00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:36.633
<i>It's honestly just a ridiculous way</i>
<i>of moving people from place to place.</i>

00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:38.700
<i>But in 1959,</i>

00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:41.633
<i>it was cars like this Triumph TR3A,</i>

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.700
<i>in pearl white </i>
<i>with black top and interior,</i>

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.133
<i>that showed the world</i>
<i>how to have your childhood</i>

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.867
<i>and your adulthood at the same time.</i>

00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:52.667
<i>It's got a four-cylinder engine,</i>

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.400
<i>105 horsepower,</i>
<i>wire wheels with knock-off hubs.</i>

00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:00.867
<i>Adults have places to go, things to do.</i>

00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:04.600
<i>Kids just want to play with something dumb</i>
<i>for no reason at all.</i>

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.667
<i>So, this is the perfect solution</i>

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.167
<i>to that eternal imperfection </i>
<i>of human life,</i>

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:14.067
<i>how to make sense and be completely silly,</i>
<i>simultaneously.</i>

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.967
You could not have
a better context for this car.

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:20.948
<i>-Which is why it's the perfect car...</i>
-It's a beautiful car.

00:01:21.048 --> 00:01:23.917
<i>-...for my special guest this week...</i>
-Bloking and crumpeting around...

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:25.400
<i>...Mr. John Oliver,</i>

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:28.622
<i>the hilarious host</i>
<i>of HBO's</i> Last Week Tonight,

00:01:28.722 --> 00:01:31.291
<i>and a perfectly logical, </i>
<i>absurd human being.</i>

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:33.427
-That's your style, and I love it.
-Yeah.

00:01:34.628 --> 00:01:36.630
[phone ringing]

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:38.999
<i>-</i>[John] <i>Hello?</i>
<i>-</i>[Jerry]<i> Hello, John?</i>

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.533
<i>-Jerry, how are you doing?</i>
<i>-Couldn't be better.</i>

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.067
[John]
<i>Is that said with sarcasm or with truth?</i>

00:01:44.171 --> 00:01:46.907
-[Jerry] <i>I abandoned the truth long ago.</i>
-[John laughs]

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:49.876
<i>-Well, we're gonna get along just fine.</i>
<i>-</i>[Jerry] <i>I'll be right there.</i>

00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:54.033
<i>I'm Jerry Seinfeld,</i>

00:01:54.081 --> 00:01:57.484
<i>and this is</i>
Comedians In Cars Getting Coffee.

00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:02.533
That's how a gentleman
greets another gentleman.

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:07.900
-What do you think?
-Well, I love it is what I think.

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:11.067
That's like the perfect British definition
of a sports car, isn't it?

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.900
-Yeah.
-Not even comfortable to get in.

00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:16.000
This is your people, okay?
This is their idea.

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:18.639
You know, it does smell
like a Triumph, though.

00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:26.000
[Jerry] We have got
a spectacularly British day today.

00:02:26.433 --> 00:02:27.933
-[John] Perfect, right?
-It's perfect.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.667
[John] It's a kind of low, gray sky
that sucks all joy from your heart.

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.921
[Jerry]
So, what do you think of it so far?

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.133
It's very cozy.

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:39.359
You can imagine yourself
on a date with, a what, a crumpet?

00:02:39.426 --> 00:02:41.495
That what you're interested in
in England, crumpets?

00:02:41.633 --> 00:02:43.700
You're blokes,
and you're in search of crumpets.

00:02:43.764 --> 00:02:46.166
-Blokes and crumpets.
-Yeah. That's all I know about England.

00:02:46.233 --> 00:02:48.500
Blokes and crumpets
feeling a lot of things,

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:50.500
-but not articulating anything.
-Right.

00:02:50.633 --> 00:02:52.300
That was basically the history

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:54.033
-of British relationships there.
-Right.

00:02:54.233 --> 00:02:58.467
"I love you, you love me, let's never
ever express that to each other.

00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:00.767
Let's die frustrated and alone."

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.900
And that is the
ultimate British love story.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.633
Good for them, they didn't act on it.

00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:07.600
How long have you been here?

00:03:07.721 --> 00:03:08.989
-Coming up on ten years.
-Ten.

00:03:09.089 --> 00:03:12.659
-And you're still saying "fru-<i>strated</i>"?
-Yeah.

00:03:12.833 --> 00:03:14.600
You haven't heard how we're talking?

00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:16.633
-What do you say?
-<i>Fru</i>-strated.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.333
No, that's... I can't do that.

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:21.567
You do hear the difference.
You just choose to ignore it.

00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:25.567
In '59, this would be great.

00:03:25.633 --> 00:03:27.633
We just got out of the Second World War,

00:03:27.733 --> 00:03:29.467
a good percentage of us died,

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.967
and now the ones that are left
are driving around in these.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.267
[Jerry] That's right.
That's exactly what happened.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.600
The war ended.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.133
-The English started making these cars...
-Yeah.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.900
And everybody thought,
"Okay, we can go back to having fun now."

00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:45.100
As much fun as we feel comfortable having.

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.067
Let's let the English

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:48.033
-show us how to have fun.
-That's true.

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:50.400
-Of any group you could pick.
-Yeah.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:52.667
Did you go to college...

00:03:52.733 --> 00:03:54.600
-Or university, I guess you'd call it?
-I did.

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:57.600
-Was that "fru-<i>strating</i>"?
-It was a little.

00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:01.767
My wife was a combat medic.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:05.033
She joined the army September 12th, 2001.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.500
She was 19.

00:04:06.633 --> 00:04:08.067
Wow. September 12th...

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:09.933
Yeah, straight after September the 11th.

00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:13.733
Well, I know September 12th
is after September 11th.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.100
How stupid do I look?

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.533
[John]
I think I was doing a gig in Pittsburgh.

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:20.533
I said it was a pretty rough gig,

00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:23.600
and there were a lot of people
collapsing drunk outside,

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.500
and I said to her,
"It was like a war zone out there."

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:29.533
And she said, "Yeah, it wasn't.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:32.167
I'm gonna give you a couple of minutes
to take that back,

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.433
because the Pittsburgh Improv,
I do not doubt, is a rough gig,

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.500
but it's not Fallujah rough."

00:04:42.133 --> 00:04:45.367
I have been watching
so much of your stuff.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.300
I really have to compliment you.

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:49.467
I'm not gonna use this
in the show, of course.

00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:55.967
Tell me about the show
because I have to say, in all honesty,

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:57.567
I'm obsessed with it now,

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.467
and I don't even know what the hell it is.

00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:00.733
Neither do I.

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:02.933
Is it just you talking? Is that it?

00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:04.300
Pretty much.

00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:05.833
[both laughing]

00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:08.567
This is a cool area.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.333
I think this is Williamsburg.

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:13.967
This used to be Orthodox Jews,
and now it's hipsters.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.267
Two versions, really, of the same thing.

00:05:16.367 --> 00:05:17.600
Let's all look alike.

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:32.667
[inaudible]

00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:34.700
[John] Wow.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.733
That is a judgmental silence
you just walked into.

00:05:39.239 --> 00:05:42.442
I wouldn't describe this as a hush
because a hush is comfortable.

00:05:42.533 --> 00:05:44.467
A hush has a softness to it.

00:05:44.633 --> 00:05:47.300
-It is judgmental...
-That's a hard silence.

00:05:48.833 --> 00:05:50.500
Welcome to my childhood.

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:55.500
Please do this gently,
do this smoothly, okay?

00:05:56.333 --> 00:06:00.400
There's a table of women behind you,
four different colors of hair,

00:06:00.533 --> 00:06:02.133
but be... be subtle, be smooth.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.033
-I've got this. Don't worry about it.
-Yeah.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.200
I was right.
We're dealing with the 'rents.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.333
In England, ladies of a certain age,

00:06:09.433 --> 00:06:11.367
when they want to
shake it up a little bit,

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:12.500
they'll go for purple.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.233
Do men have anything like that?

00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:16.167
I think, well, handsome men might.

00:06:20.267 --> 00:06:22.533
I know there's no answers
for me in a haircut.

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:24.967
Well, you did make
an adjustment to your hair.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.600
-I did, for the HBO show.
-Which I noted.

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:30.000
Before I became aware
of your comedy, I kept thinking,

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.267
<i>-"What is this guy doing with the bangs?"</i>
<i>-That's true.</i>

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.600
Because men with bangs disturb me.

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:38.131
-It's stand-up, there's different rules.
-It's different.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.167
In stand-up, you want
a mongrel-y, cretinous, troll-like...

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.500
Where the limbs are moving...

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.867
-Out-from-under-a-bridge individual.
-Exactly.

00:06:44.938 --> 00:06:47.240
-Suddenly into the daylight.
-Yeah. [laughs]

00:06:48.967 --> 00:06:51.200
Guess there's a lot of inside stuff
about England

00:06:51.267 --> 00:06:52.567
which just doesn't translate.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:58.767
Like, it became clear that as soon as
this voice started criticizing America...

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:02.433
And listen, I understand that we have done
some terrible things through our history.

00:07:02.567 --> 00:07:06.333
Nobody's really thinking
about that here. No.

00:07:06.467 --> 00:07:07.533
Nobody cares about England.

00:07:07.733 --> 00:07:10.533
So, let me disabuse you. You can relax.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.600
We don't care where you're from.

00:07:12.733 --> 00:07:15.433
-That is such a hurtful thing to say.
-You have a lovely, charming...

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.100
This little ding-a-ling... The door opens.
Ding-a-ling-a-ling.

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:20.006
-[groans]
-"Have you had a lovely day?"

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:23.900
We just think of the English as
a cute little... It's a curio shop to us.

00:07:24.033 --> 00:07:25.500
-Ding-a-ling-a-ling.
-Ding-a-ling-a-ling.

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:27.180
-"Cup of tea, mister?"
-Yeah!

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.000
You don't even seem English.

00:07:32.167 --> 00:07:35.767
You just seem like an unusual person

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.967
-who talks different...
-Uh-huh.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:41.767
...but this guy's very funny.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.233
The place that your voice comes from
does not feel English,

00:07:45.367 --> 00:07:47.033
-even with the accent...
-Right.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:48.733
And the glasses, the hair you got rid of.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:49.867
-The whole thing?
-Yeah.

00:07:49.967 --> 00:07:52.033
-And the...
-Yeah, the puppety walk...

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.633
I think that's why
your stuff is working so well.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.900
-This did not work in Britain...
-It didn't?

00:07:59.967 --> 00:08:01.333
...as well as it does here.

00:08:02.015 --> 00:08:03.984
I used to go to
the Edinburgh Festival every year.

00:08:04.467 --> 00:08:07.367
I had 100% walkout in Edinburgh.

00:08:07.433 --> 00:08:11.033
Started with six people,
two leave, like, ten minutes in.

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:14.494
Twenty minutes in, another two leave.
I'm down to two, a couple.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.900
The guy says, "I'm going to the bathroom."

00:08:17.067 --> 00:08:19.800
So, now, I'm just left with the woman.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.933
She's there with her bag,

00:08:22.667 --> 00:08:26.867
and I see her hand reach down
to her bag about 25 minutes.

00:08:27.167 --> 00:08:29.900
I said, "Are you leaving?"
She said, "Yeah."

00:08:30.667 --> 00:08:32.633
Picks up the bag, walks out.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.267
I remember the sound
of the door closing behind her.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.733
-Now I'm in an empty room...
-Right.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.167
...with a sound technician saying,
"Do you want to carry on?"

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:43.967
"No, it's over. The gig's over."

00:08:44.233 --> 00:08:46.067
-Thanks so much.
-[Jerry laughs]

00:08:46.667 --> 00:08:48.367
What about jokes about your wife?

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:49.933
How does that go down?

00:08:50.167 --> 00:08:54.600
Does she understand that these
are meaningless thoughts and words...

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:56.669
My wife, it's taken...
16 years we've been married,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.067
she finally understands
I don't mean any of this.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.600
We will put any words together.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.967
I was talking with Julia Louis-Dreyfus.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.879
She said,
"You said you would never get married."

00:09:05.945 --> 00:09:08.448
I said, "I still feel that way."
It doesn't even make sense.

00:09:08.533 --> 00:09:10.533
So... But that's obviously funny.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:12.600
So, do you pay for that comment?

00:09:12.733 --> 00:09:14.133
No, she understands now.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.200
-Now she knows.
-I don't know what I'm saying.

00:09:16.333 --> 00:09:17.833
Right. That was a sequence of words

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.567
that you knew had a laugh
at the end of it.

00:09:19.626 --> 00:09:22.028
-That's right. That's it.
-So, you said them. Yeah.

00:09:22.133 --> 00:09:24.933
You just described my entire brain.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:31.367
I did <i>Letterman</i> once,
and he was very interested in her.

00:09:31.567 --> 00:09:34.000
-Your wife?
-Yeah, he was very interested in...

00:09:34.067 --> 00:09:37.067
Like, what she'd done,
why she was with me.

00:09:37.433 --> 00:09:40.267
I think he said during that, what he said,
"Does she think you're funny?"

00:09:40.333 --> 00:09:42.000
And I said, honestly, "No."

00:09:42.167 --> 00:09:43.600
She said, "Why would you say that?"

00:09:43.667 --> 00:09:45.933
I said, "Because I'm not
your favorite comedian, right?

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.200
You like Gaffigan."

00:09:47.333 --> 00:09:49.167
"Maybe just say I am, next time."

00:09:49.467 --> 00:09:52.833
But then I was trying to explain,
"But that wouldn't be funny."

00:09:53.059 --> 00:09:57.130
-You were a comedian when she met you.
-Sure, but the internal logic of comedy

00:09:57.333 --> 00:10:00.833
is really hard for anyone
to understand from the outside.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:02.833
And what is the internal logic of comedy?

00:10:02.933 --> 00:10:05.733
That you'll do anything for a laugh,
like a sociopath.

00:10:05.833 --> 00:10:06.800
That's right.

00:10:07.433 --> 00:10:10.467
Today, I was just looking at
some of the stuff we have for this week.

00:10:10.567 --> 00:10:13.000
So, if it's something
that we want to practice

00:10:13.046 --> 00:10:16.616
to prove how easy it is to do,
like, we started this church to show

00:10:16.683 --> 00:10:18.718
the extent to which
churches are tax-exempt.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.467
-Yeah, yeah.
-That was funnier to me to then do it...

00:10:21.567 --> 00:10:24.500
And then people did it,
just send in cash the next day...

00:10:24.567 --> 00:10:27.000
-Like, just cash flying in.
-How much did you get?

00:10:27.067 --> 00:10:29.867
-$70,000. It was huge.
-And who's got that money now?

00:10:29.933 --> 00:10:31.267
-We donated it.
-All right.

00:10:32.265 --> 00:10:34.467
-Would've been funnier to buy jet skis.
-Yes!

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.933
You should have bought a jet ski
and donated that.

00:10:37.067 --> 00:10:38.867
Donate that to Doctors without Borders?

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:42.800
Yeah, two doctors on jet skis,
with the stethoscopes in the wind.

00:10:43.167 --> 00:10:44.367
That's a great look.

00:10:44.433 --> 00:10:45.933
-That's a fantastic look.
-Yeah.

00:10:46.067 --> 00:10:48.033
-Have you ever jet skied?
-Yes, sir.

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:50.000
I can't picture you on a jet ski.

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:51.533
I like to explore things.

00:10:51.633 --> 00:10:53.200
I don't expect to enjoy them,

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.667
but I am curious
what is working for other people.

00:10:55.722 --> 00:10:56.556
Right.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:01.333
Does your mind ever go

00:11:01.467 --> 00:11:03.267
to a place you would like
to take the show,

00:11:03.367 --> 00:11:06.433
or you think,
"I'd like to attempt this daily?"

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:08.433
I'm sure you get that question
all the time.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.667
-Yeah, but that's not gonna happen.
-How do you know?

00:11:10.733 --> 00:11:12.433
At the moment,
this is the platonic ideal.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:13.367
I got it.

00:11:13.467 --> 00:11:18.233
Now how long we'll be able to do that
at this pace and this level of intensity?

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.367
How long we can do that to our staff?
I don't know.

00:11:21.114 --> 00:11:24.717
This is not... When I say sustainable,
months in the future...

00:11:24.784 --> 00:11:26.786
You've heard environmental-type people
say that?

00:11:26.967 --> 00:11:28.033
So, you're using the word,

00:11:28.167 --> 00:11:31.400
but you don't really know
what it means or how to do it?

00:11:31.467 --> 00:11:34.000
No. I just know that it's a word
that comforts people.

00:11:34.067 --> 00:11:35.167
Yes.

00:11:39.433 --> 00:11:41.067
-So, you'd sprinkle it in?
-Yeah.

00:11:41.133 --> 00:11:42.833
-That's smart.
-Exactly, say sustainable

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.633
and watch people go,
"Good, we're gonna be okay, guys."

00:11:46.333 --> 00:11:49.100
-Yeah, I don't know.
-You are a devastating human being.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:53.813
HBO will let you do anything,
so you want to say, "Where is that line?

00:11:53.933 --> 00:11:56.067
Let's see what 'anything' means,
shall we?"

00:11:57.767 --> 00:12:00.167
So, I went to Russia to talk to Snowden,

00:12:00.286 --> 00:12:02.088
and we were in trouble
the whole time there,

00:12:02.155 --> 00:12:03.990
like, the Russians are mad we're there.

00:12:04.067 --> 00:12:06.400
Can only imagine the Americans
are mad that we've gone.

00:12:06.733 --> 00:12:08.600
-Right.
-And I get back home,

00:12:08.833 --> 00:12:12.500
and it was really fun because,
you know, trouble is fun.

00:12:12.633 --> 00:12:13.667
Yeah.

00:12:14.467 --> 00:12:18.100
And my wife said, "I thought you were done
with this kind of stuff."

00:12:18.667 --> 00:12:20.733
And I said, "Yeah, I don't think I'm done.

00:12:21.207 --> 00:12:22.475
I don't think I'm done."

00:12:24.433 --> 00:12:28.067
Why would she think you're done?

00:12:28.367 --> 00:12:29.567
I don't want to start trouble

00:12:29.633 --> 00:12:31.267
-between you.
-The rational brain...

00:12:31.384 --> 00:12:34.287
"You're an adult, right? Don't need to...
What's the point?

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:36.100
-What are you trying to prove?"
-You have a child.

00:12:36.167 --> 00:12:37.467
"We've got a child.

00:12:37.533 --> 00:12:39.000
-That's right, we have a baby."
-Yes.

00:12:39.067 --> 00:12:41.267
"Why are you going
to irritate the Russian government?"

00:12:41.467 --> 00:12:43.067
-"Because it's funny."
-Wow.

00:12:43.233 --> 00:12:44.900
I think it's like a kid.

00:12:45.033 --> 00:12:48.133
Like, it's funny being in trouble
in a controlled situation

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:49.733
where you're not in that much trouble.

00:12:49.867 --> 00:12:51.300
-Right.
-It's really funny.

00:12:51.533 --> 00:12:54.433
You seem like
you wouldn't mind actual trouble.

00:12:54.567 --> 00:12:58.900
I think... I wouldn't mind it
until I really encountered it.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.033
Or if it was affecting someone else.

00:13:06.367 --> 00:13:08.100
Sure. Sure, yeah.

00:13:08.321 --> 00:13:10.390
That's what you're
supposed to say, right? Yeah.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.200
-Definitely. No, definitely...
-In a sustainable way.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:15.400
Oh, good.

00:13:16.729 --> 00:13:18.731
-[Jerry] You had a good time?
-I had a great time.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:19.833
-Me, too. Great time.
-Yeah.

00:13:19.933 --> 00:13:21.500
Nothing that makes me happier than this.

00:13:21.567 --> 00:13:22.567
-Yeah.
-Nothing.

00:13:22.633 --> 00:13:24.367
-Isn't it weird?
-I'm telling you, I'm at 10.

00:13:24.433 --> 00:13:25.900
This is as comfortable as I get.

00:13:26.533 --> 00:13:28.333
You've seen me in ways

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.800
-that my loved ones don't see me.
-Right.

00:13:30.867 --> 00:13:33.800
-Because I'm just...
-You're just not there for them.

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:36.800
-You're not there for them.
-I'm as there as much as I can be.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:38.100
-As much as you can be.
-Right.

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.300
God help us.

00:13:40.067 --> 00:13:42.233
God Almighty, help us.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:48.300
[inaudible]

00:13:50.333 --> 00:13:54.233
This is a pinnacle
of English automotive culture right here.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:59.267
I mean, they really came up
with something charming, irresistible.

00:13:59.367 --> 00:14:00.833
That is just irresistible.

00:14:05.333 --> 00:14:10.667
I like the massive English study
that was done about whether you get wetter

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:15.467
if you run to get out of the rain,
or if you walk,

00:14:15.633 --> 00:14:19.333
so that you're not hit as forcibly
by the rain.

00:14:19.425 --> 00:14:22.195
-Massive study.
-What was the conclusion to that study?

00:14:23.233 --> 00:14:24.733
You get wetter walking.

00:14:25.967 --> 00:14:27.033
No [bleep].

00:14:28.833 --> 00:14:31.100
I'm glad that study was so massive.

00:14:31.233 --> 00:14:34.233
That felt like that could have been
a study of two people.

00:14:34.367 --> 00:14:36.133
-Okay, it's raining outside.
-You run.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:37.833
Walk around the block,
run around the block.

00:14:37.967 --> 00:14:39.267
-That's it.
-Guess what?

00:14:39.533 --> 00:14:42.333
The guy that got back sooner was less wet.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.400
-Yeah.
-"It's a study out of England?

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:47.200
Well, you already have my full,
undivided attention.

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:49.467
What are those hobbits
doing over there now?"

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:53.100
"Well, they wanted to decide
how you get the most wet."

00:14:53.267 --> 00:14:55.533
"Of course they did. Please continue."

00:14:55.667 --> 00:14:56.833
"One walked, one ran."

00:14:57.029 --> 00:15:01.801
"Did he run, or was it a kind of
bizarre jog he threw down?"

00:15:01.967 --> 00:15:03.100
Well, good.

00:15:03.269 --> 00:15:04.370
That's what England does

00:15:04.437 --> 00:15:06.973
now that they don't control
any of the world anymore.

00:15:08.708 --> 00:15:14.514
My first key lime pie,
I ate off a tray outside a hotel room

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:17.233
-in a corridor in Houston.
-Right.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:18.667
-It was delicious.
-Yeah.

00:15:18.867 --> 00:15:21.233
There was two-thirds of it left,
and I ate all of it.

00:15:22.667 --> 00:15:25.167
I was wondering whether they'd walk out
in the morning and say,

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:26.900
"Hold on a second,

00:15:27.167 --> 00:15:29.400
what human animal

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:34.033
ate two-thirds of my key lime pie?"

00:15:34.133 --> 00:15:36.267
-So, you stood there eating it?
-No, I took it.

00:15:36.367 --> 00:15:38.033
-I took it into my room...
-Yeah.

00:15:38.467 --> 00:15:41.233
...ate it, felt zero regret...

00:15:41.767 --> 00:15:43.933
-...put the plate back outside...
-Right.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:45.867
...and walked away confidently,

00:15:46.133 --> 00:15:48.933
thinking nothing about that seemed
like a bad decision.

00:15:50.533 --> 00:15:54.900
I would eat a roll off a room service tray
in a hallway of a hotel

00:15:55.033 --> 00:15:57.167
well into my successful years.

00:15:57.233 --> 00:15:59.100
-Of course!
-Who would think...

00:15:59.158 --> 00:16:02.829
"Hey, let's poison this roll in case
there's a comic coming home at 3:00 a.m."

00:16:02.895 --> 00:16:04.564
-That's not happening.
-"We can kill him."

00:16:04.667 --> 00:16:07.167
What a security video that would be.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:08.900
"NBC's Seinfeld

00:16:09.933 --> 00:16:14.267
eating a roll off the floor
on his knees like a dog."

00:16:17.467 --> 00:16:19.200
I'm not really interested in acting.

00:16:19.267 --> 00:16:21.067
-I did this one sitcom.
-Yeah.

00:16:21.167 --> 00:16:22.967
-I did this one dramatic scene.
-Right.

00:16:23.067 --> 00:16:25.467
So, we're in a car just like this,
and we're really close,

00:16:25.867 --> 00:16:27.600
and she starts crying in this scene.

00:16:27.833 --> 00:16:29.800
And the only thing I'm thinking,
watching her, is,

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:32.200
"You're doing some amazing acting
right now."

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.133
-But she's getting nothing back from me.
-Nothing.

00:16:35.233 --> 00:16:39.100
Just an audience member half-a-foot away
from her face, going, "That's good.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:42.267
You're really crying, aren't you?
Those are actual tears on your face..."

00:16:42.333 --> 00:16:46.200
If you ever catch a rerun of my TV series,
and you see me in any scene,

00:16:46.733 --> 00:16:48.400
that is exactly what I'm thinking.

00:16:49.567 --> 00:16:51.233
"I mean, wow, look at this guy acting."

00:16:52.367 --> 00:16:55.500
"Look at this.
Look at the commitment on that face."

00:16:55.548 --> 00:16:57.416
"Wait, they're really into this."

00:16:58.367 --> 00:16:59.533
"Oh, it's my line now."

00:17:02.267 --> 00:17:05.767
I was never ever there,
not unlike your marriage.

00:17:08.567 --> 00:17:11.367
-[John] You gave them 100%.
-100%.

00:17:11.464 --> 00:17:16.168
I'm here. I'm giving you everything I got.
I'm not leaving anything on the field.

00:17:16.267 --> 00:17:19.367
You're getting my absolute best here.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:24.567
[Jerry] This is really great.
I think we're gonna be friends.

00:17:26.212 --> 00:17:27.480
[John laughing]

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:33.567
-[Jerry] <i>Which was bigger? Trump?</i>
-[John] <i>Trump.</i>

00:17:33.867 --> 00:17:35.700
-<i>That thing got out of hand.</i>
-[Jerry] <i>Yeah.</i>

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:40.133
I think he has a big clown-y body, too,
that's kind of... It's squidgy.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:42.967
It's something...
A kid would want to squeeze it, you know?

00:17:43.133 --> 00:17:45.167
-That's right, to relieve stress.
-Yeah.

00:17:45.298 --> 00:17:48.734
"I'm feeling tense... That's better.
That's better, I don't know why."

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:51.867
Yeah, he would make
a good little stress ball.

00:17:51.933 --> 00:17:53.067
Yeah.

00:17:53.205 --> 00:17:56.475
He would make a better stress ball
than a president, that's for sure.

00:18:01.133 --> 00:18:02.133
And we're out.